  "prompt": "You are an associate analyst at Brennan Brothers & Co. in the equity research group. Kara Vossberg, our managing director, needs a budget-vs-actual variance analysis workbook on Pfizer Inc. (PFE) following the SCHEDULE 13G/A filing on 2026-05-12. The analysis covers PFE's most recent quarterly results for the period ending 2026-03-29. The attached file, PFE_historical_financials.xlsx, contains five-year historical financials to support prior-period comparisons.\n\nBuild the workbook in Excel using the following structure and requirements:\n\n1. Create a primary worksheet tab named \"Variance Analysis\" with a complete income statement comparison for Q1 2026. Pull actual Revenues of $14.451B, Cost of Goods and Services Sold of $3.548B, Selling General & Administrative expense of $2.961B, and Net Income of $2.687B from the EDGAR XBRL filing. Show these against Q1 2025 actuals (sourced from the historical file) and consensus estimates where available, with dollar and percentage variances clearly labeled. Use the 2023 annual RevenueFromContractWithCustomerExcludingAssessedTax figure of $50.914B as the full-year baseline reference where annual context is helpful.\n\n2. Calculate gross margin, operating margin, and net margin for the quarter and present the variance from prior periods. Break out COGS and SG&A as separate line items with a brief note on any variance driver where the change exceeds ±10%.\n\n3. Build a balance sheet summary tab with Assets ($207.618B), Liabilities ($117.214B), Stockholders' Equity ($90.101B), and Cash and Cash Equivalents ($1.703B) compared against prior quarter or year-end figures from the historical file. Include a debt-to-equity ratio and current ratio where the data supports it.\n\n4. Report diluted EPS of $0.47 per share with variance analysis against the prior period and consensus. Ensure EPS ties to the net income and share count in the income statement tab.\n\n5. Create a one-tab executive summary highlighting the top five largest absolute dollar variances with a brief narrative explanation for each. This should read as client-facing — Kara may forward it directly to the investment committee.\n\n6. Add a \"Data Sources\" tab that cites the EDGAR XBRL fact ID for every number pulled from the filing (e.g., Revenues, CostOfGoodsAndServicesSold, NetIncomeLoss, EarningsPerShareDiluted, Assets, Liabilities, StockholdersEquity, CashAndCashEquivalentsAtCarryingValue, SellingGeneralAndAdministrativeExpense).\n\n7. Formatting: all monetary values in millions with one decimal place and thousands separator. Use standard IB color-coding — blue font for hard-coded inputs, black for formulas, green for source links. Negative variances in red or parentheses. Flag any line with a ±10%+ variance using a comment or highlight column. Set column widths and print areas so each tab prints cleanly on a single landscape page. All calculations must be formula-driven with ROUND functions to avoid floating-point display issues, and every figure must reconcile to the pinned EDGAR facts with no rounding discrepancy greater than $0.1M.\n\nOrganize tabs left-to-right in the order Kara would review: Summary, Variance Analysis, Balance Sheet, Data Sources.",
